\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{The Gaussian partition function and probability law}
\date{2026-10-25}

\p{Let #{Q} be a [positive precision parameter](fdip-0004) on a
finite real inner-product space #{(E,h)}, with canonical volume
#{\lambda_E}. Its \newvocab{Gaussian partition function} is
##{Z_Q=\int_E w_Q\,d\lambda_E,
\qquad w_Q(x)=e^{-h(x,Qx)/2}.}
The [normalization theorem](fdip-002T) proves
##{0<Z_Q=\frac{(2\pi)^{n/2}}{\sqrt{\det Q}}<\infty,
\qquad n=\dim E.}
Its \newvocab{Gaussian probability law} is the Borel measure
##{\mu_Q(A)=Z_Q^{-1}\int_A w_Q\,d\lambda_E.}
In the counting field specialization this is exactly the density
#{Z_Q^{-1}\exp(-\phi^TQ\phi/2)} against the product reference measure.}
\proof{
\p{The weight is positive Borel. Nonnegativity and the empty-set axiom
follow from the nonnegative integral. For pairwise disjoint Borel
#{A_k}, the functions #{w_Q\sum_{k=1}^N\mathbf1_{A_k}} increase to
#{w_Q\mathbf1_{\bigcup_k A_k}}. Monotone convergence and finite
additivity of the integral give countable additivity after division
by #{Z_Q}. The total mass is #{Z_Q/Z_Q=1}.}
}
\p{In an orthonormal eigenchart the density is the product of the centered
scalar densities #{I(q_i)^{-1}e^{-q_i y_i^2/2}}. This identification
uses the scalar value and normalization, before computing covariance.
For #{n=0}, the action is zero at the unique point, #{Z_Q=1}, and
#{\mu_Q=\delta_0}. Replacing the reference volume by #{c\lambda_E},
#{0<c<\infty}, multiplies #{Z_Q} by #{c} and leaves #{\mu_Q}
unchanged, as in the [reference-rescaling example](fdip-002N).
A finite-sum \vocabk{partition function}{ftip-008J} has the same
normalizing role; finite support alone does not prove integrability here.}
\p{The density convention is compared with
\citet{Lemma B.54 and Proposition B.55, pp. 504-505}{friedli2017statistical};
that source uses covariance rather than precision. Countable additivity
uses \citet{Theorem 1.4.44, pp. 107-108, preliminary version}{tao2011measure}.
The [normalization failures](fdip-002U) distinguish infinite normalizers,
zero weights and the zero-dimensional case.}
